<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    SYSTEM DPI awareness, deliberately not PerMonitorV2. Do not "fix" this without reading the
    whole comment - three per-monitor builds have already been shipped and rejected.

    WHY PER-MONITOR CANNOT WORK HERE (verified against slint 1.17.1 / winit 0.30.13 sources):

    1. Slint never resizes on a scale-factor change. Its winit backend dispatches the event and
       throws away winit's inner_size_writer, with a TODO saying as much
       (i-slint-backend-winit/event_loop.rs:490-501). The handler only calls set_scale_factor
       (i-slint-core/api.rs:734-737), unlike the Resized path next to it, which also updates the
       window-item geometry and the renderer. So after a DPI change the scale factor is NEW while
       the cached physical size and layout geometry are OLD.

    2. That desync is fatal for a software renderer specifically. The scene is sized from the new
       scale factor (i-slint-renderer-software/lib.rs:568-583) while the softbuffer surface is
       sized from the stale physical size (backend-winit/renderer/sw.rs:86,103-105). Softbuffer
       blits 1:1 on Win32 and never stretches, so the two disagreeing can only show up as content
       drawn at the wrong size and clipped. Only an OS-driven WM_SIZE reconciles the two, because
       resize_event (winitwindowadapter.rs:823-855) is the single place the cached size is written.

    3. Our own frameless drag makes it worse. drag_self() calls a blocking SendMessageW with
       WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN from inside a Slint callback, which holds winit's event handler for the
       whole modal move loop. Dragging across a monitor boundary therefore BUFFERS the DPI event:
       Slint gets nothing for the entire drag and only reconciles on mouse-up. Since dragging
       between monitors is the exact action in question, this alone rules the approach out.

    4. winit 0.30.13 also nudges the window back onto the old monitor mid-drag, because
       MonitorFromWindow still reports the old one (winit windows/event_loop.rs:2349), growing the
       window a step per bounce. Fixed upstream by winit PR #4341 (merged 2025-08-24) but released
       only in 0.31.0-beta; the 0.30.x backport PR #4637 was closed unmerged. Slint pins
       winit ^0.30.2, so there is no upgrade path at any layer. slint-ui/slint#11073 is open and
       labelled upstream; slint 1.17.1 is the newest release and still contains the TODO above.

    WHAT WAS ACTUALLY TRIED, all rejected in the field:
      - v1.0.2/v1.0.3: plain PerMonitorV2, no app-side code at all. Window changed size oddly when
        dragged between screens, and flashed black or transparent.
      - v1.0.4: swallowed WM_DPICHANGED. This deletes winit's corrective SetWindowPos and so
        deletes the only WM_SIZE, making the desync permanent: content drawn at the wrong scale.
      - v1.0.6: let the message through, then forced the frame to the rect Windows supplies. A
        third SetWindowPos on top of winit's, which corrects the HWND without reconciling Slint's
        cached size: content offset and clipped past the right edge.

    With system awareness the window is never asked to rescale, so none of the above is reachable.
    Windows composites it for the other screen instead, which costs a little sharpness on a monitor
    whose scaling differs from the system setting. Setting both monitors to the same Windows
    scaling removes even that. Revisit only once slint#11073 is fixed upstream AND the drag in
    point 3 no longer blocks winit's event loop.

    Two things that look like levers and are not: SLINT_SCALE_FACTOR is honoured
    (winitwindowadapter.rs:461-467) but freezes the physical size, and gdiScaling applies only to
    DPI-unaware processes and cannot scale an application-constructed bitmap, which is exactly what
    softbuffer hands to Windows.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>
</assembly>
